            <ArticleTitle>The effect of anesthesia method on serum level of pro-brain natriuretic Peptide in patients undergoing orthopedic surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e19707</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5812/aapm.19707</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Surgical stress response is among the most severe stress tolerated by the patient, which needs suppression by anesthesia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We assessed the effect of three methods of anesthesia on postoperative levels of pro-brain natriuretic peptide (pro-BNP) to determine the most effective one in preventing surgical stress response.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In a randomized clinical trial, 120 patients who were 18 to 65 years old and met inclusion and exclusion criteria were selected and randomly allocated to three groups of 40:Group A, general anesthesia plus epidural catheter; Group B, general anesthesia and intravenous patient-controlled analgesia; and Group C, spinal anesthesia plus intravenous patient-controlled analgesia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There was no difference between three groups for basic characteristics and variables and baseline pro-BNP levels; however, postoperative pro-BNP levels in Groups A, B, and C were respectively63.8 ± 10.1, 83.2 ± 12.3, and 51.5 ± 8.5 ng/L (ANOVA, P = 0.01).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The results of the current study suggested that spinal anesthesia plus intravenous patient-controlled analgesia have the most favorable cardiac effects regarding postoperative levels of pro-BNP.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
